---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const A1111 = 'https://github.com/AUTOMATIC1111/stable-diffusion-webui/blob/master/modules/images.py';
const COMFY = 'https://github.com/comfyanonymous/ComfyUI/blob/master/nodes.py';
const COMFY_IMAGES = 'https://github.com/comfyanonymous/ComfyUI/blob/master/comfy_extras/nodes_images.py';
const INVOKE = 'https://invoke-ai.github.io/InvokeAI-7/development/architecture/media-metadata/';
const FOOOCUS = 'https://github.com/lllyasviel/Fooocus/blob/main/modules/private_logger.py';
const FLUX = 'https://github.com/black-forest-labs/flux/blob/main/src/flux/util.py';
const IPTC = 'https://cv.iptc.org/newscodes/digitalsourcetype/';
const SPEC = 'https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.2/specs/C2PA_Specification.html';
const MS = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-foundry/openai/concepts/content-credentials';
const BFL = 'https://help.bfl.ai/articles/3705384267-c2pa-in-flux-generated-images';
const SDXL = 'https://github.com/Stability-AI/generative-models/blob/main/scripts/demo/streamlit_helpers.py';
const GOOGLE = 'https://blog.google/technology/ai/ai-image-verification-gemini-app/';
const DIFFUSERS = 'https://github.com/huggingface/diffusers/blob/main/src/diffusers/pipelines/stable_diffusion_xl/pipeline_stable_diffusion_xl.py';
---
<Base
  lang="en"
  heading="What metadata do AI images contain?"
  title="What Metadata Do AI Images Contain? — PromptWipe"
  description="Prompt, seed, model, ComfyUI workflow, C2PA: what AUTOMATIC1111, Forge, ComfyUI, InvokeAI, Fooocus and Flux write into image files, from their source code."
>
  <p class="lead">
    Local AI tools often write the full prompt, the seed, the model and every setting into the image file, and ComfyUI
    stores the whole workflow. Some hosted services document C2PA Content Credentials instead. This guide shows, from each
    tool’s source code, what goes where. Removing metadata does not remove invisible watermarks in the pixels.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Which AI tools write the prompt into the image?</h2>
    <p>
      AUTOMATIC1111, Forge, ComfyUI and InvokeAI do it by default, Fooocus does it when its metadata option is on, and
      so does the reference Flux code from Black Forest Labs. Anyone who gets the original file can read the prompt and
      the settings.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>What each tool writes, read in its source code or documentation</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Format</th><th>Where</th><th>What</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><a href={A1111}>AUTOMATIC1111</a>, Forge</td>
            <td>PNG</td>
            <td>Text chunk <code>parameters</code></td>
            <td>Prompt, negative prompt, steps, sampler, CFG scale, seed, size, model hash and name, LoRA and extension settings</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AUTOMATIC1111, Forge</td>
            <td>JPEG, WebP, AVIF</td>
            <td>EXIF <code>UserComment</code></td>
            <td>The same text</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><a href={COMFY}>ComfyUI</a></td>
            <td>PNG</td>
            <td>Text chunks <code>prompt</code> and <code>workflow</code>, plus any extra key added by nodes</td>
            <td>The whole graph as JSON: prompts, seeds, model and LoRA file names, node settings, sometimes local file paths</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><a href={COMFY_IMAGES}>ComfyUI</a></td>
            <td>AVIF, animated WebP</td>
            <td>EXIF tags holding <code>prompt:</code> and <code>workflow:</code> text</td>
            <td>The same JSON</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><a href={INVOKE}>InvokeAI</a></td>
            <td>PNG</td>
            <td>Text chunks <code>invokeai_metadata</code>, <code>invokeai_graph</code>, <code>invokeai_workflow</code> (older versions: <code>sd-metadata</code>)</td>
            <td>Generation settings, graph and workflow</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><a href={FOOOCUS}>Fooocus</a></td>
            <td>PNG</td>
            <td>Text chunks <code>parameters</code> and <code>fooocus_scheme</code></td>
            <td>Prompt, seed, models, styles</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fooocus</td>
            <td>JPEG, WebP</td>
            <td>EXIF <code>UserComment</code> (parameters), <code>Software</code> (“Fooocus v…”), <code>MakerNote</code> (scheme)</td>
            <td>The same data</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><a href={FLUX}>Flux reference code</a></td>
            <td>JPEG</td>
            <td>EXIF <code>ImageDescription</code>, <code>Software</code>, <code>Make</code>, <code>Model</code></td>
            <td>The prompt in ImageDescription; “AI generated;txt2img;flux”, “Black Forest Labs” and the model name in the others</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Two details matter for privacy. A ComfyUI workflow is a complete recipe: dropping the PNG back into ComfyUI loads
      it, with your custom nodes and file names. And Fooocus also writes a <code>log.html</code> file next to the
      images, with the prompts, which stays on your disk.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pages on <a href="/remove-stable-diffusion-metadata/">Stable Diffusion metadata</a> and on the
      <a href="/remove-comfyui-workflow-from-image/">ComfyUI workflow</a> explain how to read, remove or stop writing
      these fields.
    </p>

    <h2>Where is metadata stored in an image file?</h2>
    <p>
      In blocks set aside from the pixels, which every image format allows. Four kinds carry what AI tools write: PNG
      text chunks, EXIF, XMP and C2PA manifests.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>PNG text chunks</strong> (<code>tEXt</code>, <code>zTXt</code>, <code>iTXt</code>) are pairs of a key
          and a text, compressed or not. Any program can add one with any key, which is why each AI tool picked its own
          names.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>EXIF</strong> comes from cameras: maker, model, date, exposure, GPS position. AI tools reuse some of
          its fields, such as <code>UserComment</code>, <code>ImageDescription</code>, <code>Software</code> or
          <code>MakerNote</code>, to store their settings in JPEG and WebP files.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>XMP</strong> is an XML packet used by Adobe software and many others. It can hold the creator tool
          and the IPTC <a href={IPTC}>digital source type</a>, a standard way to say how an image was made:
          <code>trainedAlgorithmicMedia</code> means “created using generative AI” and
          <code>compositeWithTrainedAlgorithmicMedia</code> “edited using generative AI”.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>C2PA</strong> manifests, also called Content Credentials, are signed records of how the image was
          made, stored as JUMBF data (<a href={`${SPEC}#_embedding_manifests_into_non_bmff_based_assets`}>C2PA 2.2,
          annex A</a>). The <a href="/remove-c2pa-content-credentials/">C2PA page</a> explains what they contain and
          when to keep them.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Photos add their own layer: camera, lens, date and often the GPS position. See
      <a href="/remove-photo-metadata-gps/">photo metadata and GPS</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What do hosted AI generators write?</h2>
    <p>
      The vendors that document it describe C2PA Content Credentials saying that the image was made by AI, by which
      service and when, rather than the prompt. Two examples are published:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={MS}>Microsoft</a>: images from Azure OpenAI carry a manifest signed by Microsoft, with the description
        “AI Generated Image”, a software agent such as “Azure OpenAI DALL-E” or “Azure OpenAI ImageGen”, and the time
        of creation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={BFL}>Black Forest Labs</a>: JPEG and PNG images from the FLUX API carry Content Credentials with an
        AI-generated flag, the claim generator, the UTC time, and the model name and version, and no prompt, negative
        prompt, API key, account ID or IP address.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For ChatGPT, Midjourney, Gemini and Firefly, we have not inspected a real file yet. Published reports differ, and
      some come from sellers of removal tools, so this guide states nothing about them. We publish a page for a
      generator only after inspecting a real file from it.
    </p>

    <h2>Does removing metadata remove AI watermarks?</h2>
    <p>
      No. An invisible watermark is written into the pixels themselves, not into a metadata block, so no metadata tool
      removes it, PromptWipe included. Visible logos are part of the picture too.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>SynthID</strong>: <a href={GOOGLE}>Google says</a> SynthID embeds imperceptible signals in AI-generated
        content, and that the Gemini app can check an uploaded image for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>DWT-DCT</strong>, from the open-source <code>invisible-watermark</code> package: Stability AI’s
        <a href={SDXL}>SDXL reference scripts</a> call it, the <a href={FLUX}>Flux reference code</a> calls it on every
        image it saves, and the <a href={DIFFUSERS}>diffusers SDXL pipeline</a> adds it by default when the package is
        installed.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So an image can carry no metadata at all and still be recognised as AI-generated. PromptWipe is a privacy tool
      for what the file says about you, not a way to hide how an image was made.
    </p>

    <h2>How can you check what your own image contains?</h2>
    <p>
      Open it in the <a href="/view-ai-image-prompt/">PromptWipe viewer</a>: it lists every block of the file and shows
      the prompts, settings, EXIF fields and Content Credentials it finds, without sending the image anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep in mind that a copied, pasted or screenshotted image has often lost its metadata already, and that many
      social platforms re-encode uploads, which often drops it too. To know what a file you are about to share
      carries, check that file itself, then share the cleaned copy.
    </p>

    <p class="sources">
      Sources:
      <a href={A1111}>AUTOMATIC1111 images.py</a>;
      <a href={COMFY}>ComfyUI nodes.py</a> and <a href={COMFY_IMAGES}>nodes_images.py</a>;
      <a href={INVOKE}>InvokeAI, media metadata</a>;
      <a href={FOOOCUS}>Fooocus private_logger.py</a>;
      <a href={FLUX}>Flux util.py</a>;
      <a href={IPTC}>IPTC digital source type</a>;
      <a href={SPEC}>C2PA 2.2 specification</a>;
      <a href={MS}>Microsoft Learn</a>;
      <a href={BFL}>Black Forest Labs help</a>;
      <a href={GOOGLE}>Google, image verification in Gemini</a>;
      <a href={SDXL}>Stability AI generative-models</a>;
      <a href={DIFFUSERS}>diffusers</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
